Rotation About the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049491 #edb/ref 1033 3632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049491 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f90d8601-e439-5cfc-9a0b-afc8462d4f0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429734 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429735 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429736 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429737 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429738 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429739 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429740 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429741 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1031 "The Arc Length of a Parametric Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1033 997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050860 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6c427c49-4a33-55da-a8ed-0f9ab82b1d58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129073 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129073 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = t-\\sin t, \\quad y = 1-\\cos t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "066f76be-83f6-52fd-ab3f-00786d18f64e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129073 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=\\pi.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\sin^2 t +\\cos^2 t= 1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129074 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129074 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = \\sin t, \\quad y = e^t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7f404a3-8885-5a33-9cfb-1e12a6c745a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129074 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129075 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129075 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = 1+3t, \\quad y = 2t^{3/2}, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "205f4d6b-affa-530e-979b-a394e7a46bd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129075 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9(1+t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u = 1+t.$ Then, $1+3t=3u-2$ and $\\textrm{d}u = \\textrm d t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3u-2)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{6{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{4{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{6({2}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{4({2}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{24\\sqrt{2}}{5}-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}+\\frac{2}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}(16\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1039 "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129318 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129319 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129319 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129319 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129319 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1759